## 53. 3D Direction Reasoning

The same concept can be extended from 2D to 3D.

Represent:

```text
(x, y, z)
```

For example:

```text
5 m north
3 m east
2 m upward
```

becomes:

```text
(3,5,2)
```

Distance:

```text
d = √(3² + 5² + 2²)
  = √38
```

This is useful for:

```text
robotics
games
3D environments
drones
architecture
physics
```

## 54. Coordinate Transformations

A complex AI may use multiple coordinate frames:

```text
world frame
agent frame
camera frame
screen frame
```

The system must explicitly know which frame an observation belongs to.

Conceptually:

```text
world coordinates
      ↓
agent coordinates
      ↓
camera coordinates
      ↓
screen coordinates
```

This is essential for robotics and computer vision.

## 55. Spatial Proof Trace

For reliability, retain the derivation.

Example:

```text
A = (0,0)

B = A + (0,3)
  = (0,3)

C = B + (4,0)
  = (4,3)

C - A = (4,3)

distance = √(4² + 3²)
         = 5

direction = NE
```

The AI can then explain exactly how it reached the answer.

## 56. Verification

Spatial calculations can be verified using reverse operations.

If:

```text
A + displacement = B
```

then:

```text
B - displacement = A
```

Example:

```text
A = (2,3)
displacement = (5,-1)

B = (7,2)
```

Verify:

```text
B - (5,-1)
= (7-5, 2-(-1))
= (2,3)
```

The result is consistent.

## 57. Triangle Inequality Check

For any real route:

```text
straight-line distance <= path distance
```

For example:

```text
path distance = 16 km
straight-line distance = 8.94 km
```

is valid.

If a system produces:

```text
straight-line distance = 20 km
path distance = 16 km
```

then a calculation or interpretation error exists.

This is an excellent sanity check.

## 58. Complete Example

Input:

> “A person starts at a point, walks 6 km north, then 8 km east, then 2 km south. How far are they from the starting point and in which direction?”

### Step 1 — Parse

```text
6 km north
8 km east
2 km south
```

### Step 2 — Convert

```text
N6 = (0,6)
E8 = (8,0)
S2 = (0,-2)
```

### Step 3 — Add

```text
(0,6) + (8,0) + (0,-2)
= (8,4)
```

### Step 4 — Distance

```text
d = √(8² + 4²)
  = √80
  = 4√5
  ≈ 8.94 km
```

### Step 5 — Direction

Both coordinates are positive:

```text
x > 0
y > 0
```

Therefore:

```text
North-East
```

### Step 6 — Path Distance

```text
6 + 8 + 2 = 16 km
```

Final:

```text
Straight-line distance ≈ 8.94 km
Direction = North-East
Total distance traveled = 16 km
```

## 59. Example With Turns

Input:

> “A person faces east. They walk 4 km, turn left and walk 3 km, then turn right and walk 2 km.”

Initial:

```text
position = (0,0)
heading = East
```

Walk:

```text
position = (4,0)
```

Turn left:

```text
heading = North
```

Walk:

```text
position = (4,3)
```

Turn right:

```text
heading = East
```

Walk:

```text
position = (6,3)
```

Final displacement:

```text
(6,3)
```

Distance:

```text
√(6² + 3²)
= √45
≈ 6.71 km
```

Direction:

```text
North-East
```
